\tr{\td{seeded faults in a review copy (section 5.2)}\td{each of seven faults is seen by a row, and \code{SHostileEagerTest} fails on each; it passes without a fault}}
}

\p{The mutant counts differ from those of the release (agree 15,741, declined 2,034) because of the tree, not of this patch: with a rejection of the machine final, 351 mutants that the machine used to decline are rejected by it. \strong{Two things went wrong in the gating and are named here.} The first complete run of the gates was made on a build whose marks depended on the host depth bound; the convention part of the machine's gate found it (section 5.4), and all gates were repeated on the repaired build - the results above are of that build, whose jars are the sources of the patch (the same digest as a fresh build of the patched tree). And for nine minutes my own accept and differential runs were pinned to the measurement cores while my own guard window ran on Mathlib; the two affected rounds are discarded and the window was repeated (section 7), and the differential runs that were left were made on the other cores.}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. The other decline rules that touch eager reduction}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The K rule after the application of a let variable} (finding E1-F6) declines a declaration whether or not it is in eager reduction. It is the class of another package of this day and is not touched here; the units it declines are in the "declined, other" column of section 6.}
\li{\strong{"Conversion under eager reduction depends on the mode"} is the text of the rule M6-F26. It is still what a machine WITHOUT the switch \code{eager} answers (the lanes of the machine's gate that run other switch sets, the generic tier) and what a strict engine answers. In the default set it no longer occurs. Making it unconditional - the in-place comparison in every switch set - is a change of what "no switch" means and was not made.}
\li{\strong{The loader's rule}: a declaration is declined when a metadata node stands around an application of \code{eagerReduce}. Officially such a node hides the application from the test of \code{\verb~|infer_app~}, so the argument is compared in PLAIN mode; the loader drops metadata nodes, the engine would see an eager argument and could reject what the official checker accepts (the row \code{s3-mdata-eager-hidden-ok}, the only row of the family that is still declined, by the loader). With the comparison in place this needs nothing from the engine but one bit of the loader: "this argument reads as \code{\verb~|eagerReduce _ _~} only because a node was dropped". An argument with that bit is an ordinary plain argument, which is the official treatment, and the declaration is decided. Where to carry the bit (a flag of the application node that the two tests \code{\verb~|Infer.isEagerReduce~} and \code{\verb~|Code.isEagerReduce~} read) is the loader's and the store's decision; nothing of it is in this patch.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{10. The argument for exactness, and what it does not cover}

\p{\strong{The claim.} For a declaration checked with the switch in its default mode the machine gives the official verdict, under the machine's standing assumption that the official procedure on terms and the machine's on values ask the same comparisons up to instantiation, beta and zeta - and with the exceptions below, each of which is an ACCEPT of a derivable statement where the official checker rejects.}

\p{\strong{The argument.} (1) Under the bit the machine runs the official order, with the bit read where the official flag is read and no shortcut of plain mode. (2) A table read crosses the modes in three ways. An entry that is not local was written by a comparison that passed no point where the modes part: its computation is the same in both modes, so the reader gets what it would have computed. A local entry of this declaration is what the official caches hold: the official kernel computed the same pair in this declaration and gives the cached answer to either mode. A local entry of another declaration is not read: the official caches of another declaration are gone. (3) An entry that the official cache holds and the pair table does not have under the records in hand is found by expression (rule 5), and an entry that the machine never wrote because it knew the term as checked is written by the run alone (rule 7). (4) Reductions and types do not depend on the mode after rule 6, so their memo words are right in both.}

\p{\strong{What it does not cover.} Every item is an accept of a derivable statement (kind T in the sense of [the divergence classes](design-official-divergence)), except the last four, which are stated as risks.}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The K rule} (rule 6): the machine reduces a K recursor under the bit when the plain comparison licenses it; the official kernel leaves it stuck unless its cache holds the reduct. Four rows: \code{krule-eager-alone-bad}, \code{krule-memo-across-declarations-eager-bad}, \code{krule-memo-across-binders-eager-bad}, \code{e1-skip-identity-both-bad}. The alternative, leaving the recursor stuck, gives the official verdict on the first of them and rejects \code{e1-skip-krule-plain-redex-then-eager-ok}, which the official checker accepts; the two cannot be told apart on values.}
\li{\strong{Binder instances.} The machine's variables are canonical and its table of checked terms does not tell two instances of a binder apart, so a local entry outlives the binders of its declaration: a pair compared in plain mode under one instance answers under the flag under the next. Three rows: \code{canon-fvar-pos-across-binders-eager-bad}, \code{e1-bad-pair-subterm-eager-bad}, \code{mo3-binder-siblings-at-depth-two-eager-bad}. It is the eager sibling of the plain-mode row \code{s3-wc-canon-fvar-bad} of [the review of the default set](rep-soundness-default-set). A version of the patch that ended a local entry when a variable of its pair was bound again gave the official verdict on these three and rejected an official accept (section 5.4).}
\li{\strong{A pair that a shortcut of plain mode hides.} A plain comparison that the same-head probe, closure congruence or a skipped proof position answers without looking at a pair whose modes differ is not marked (the marks before the two probes see a Nat function at the head of the pair in hand, not deeper); its entry is not local, and a later declaration reads it under the flag. One row: \code{mo3-proofpos-pair-in-definitions-plain-then-eager-bad}. Without the two shortcuts (the set of wave 6 with \code{eager}) the row gets the official verdict. Marking every answer of a shortcut on an open pair would close it and make most entries of plain mode local; it was not built.}
\li{\strong{A dishonest prelude.} The argument uses that the official Nat functions fold to what they unfold to (no pair was found that is true under the flag and false in plain mode: the rows \code{mo3-mod-*}). An export that declares a Nat function with another body was not attacked here; it is the subject of the prelude guards.}
\li{\strong{Risk: the search by expression is bounded} (256 nodes per side of a pair, 2^22 nodes per declaration). A hostile declaration whose cached pair is two large terms under other records than the ones in hand is answered by the eager procedure: a rejection where the official cache accepts. Not demonstrated.}
\li{\strong{Risk: a comparison the machine skips and the official kernel makes}, other than through its table of checked terms and other than in the K rule: two terms that are one value are not compared by the machine, and what the official kernel caches while it compares them is unknown to it. For the K rule this is rule 6; for the success cache the sub-pairs of two terms that are one value are pairs of terms that are one value, which the machine answers by identity. Argued, not demonstrated.}
\li{\strong{Risk: resources.} Under the bit no shortcut helps: a comparison under the flag that only a shortcut makes feasible would end at a limit, which is a decline and never a verdict. None on the eight corpora (the tick counts are the same).}
\li{\strong{Risk: the marks are an over-approximation by a cheap test}, complete only as far as the two official places are the only places where the modes part. A point that is missed makes a mode-dependent entry an entry of the session: an accept in another declaration, not a rejection.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{11. What an adversarial reviewer must still attack}

\ul{
\li{\strong{Rule 7 as the only net under a rejection.} With a rejection of the machine final by default ([the package on the re-check](rep-MO-1)), a rejection after an eager-only event rests on the run alone. Attack: a declaration whose first run is ACCEPTED through a table read that the run alone would not have (the reverse direction is covered: the first run rejects, the second decides); a rejection WITHOUT an eager-only event in a declaration that enters eager reduction (the argument says the computation is then the plain one; rows with a failing same-head comparison written under the bit and read in plain mode).}
\li{\strong{The mark of a comparison} (\code{\verb~|Conv.convScoped~}): an entry that is not local although its comparison depended on a marked one. The cases argued and covered by rows or by the gate: a comparison that completes in its host frame, one that is suspended (the frame \code{\verb~|CONV2[11]~}; the convention part of the machine's gate compares the pair entries under four host depth bounds), a speculative segment that is cut in host frames and one that is cut by the driver after a suspension. The last has no row of its own: a cut segment whose comparisons were suspended, in a declaration with a mode-dependent pair. The seeded faults of section 5.2 dropped the inheritance as a whole, not one of these paths.}
\li{\strong{The search by expression}: pairs under other records than the plain comparison's (a let, a beta redex, a definition that unfolds to the pair, a far frame), and the bound.}
\li{\strong{The K rule} with rule 6: the asymmetric cases (a comparison that is true under the flag and false in plain mode needs a dishonest Nat function), nested K comparisons under the bit (the mark of an event is one bit for the nest), a cut inside the K comparison.}
\li{\strong{Elaborated code}: the eager argument checked by \code{\verb~|Code.targConv~} (the frame that restores the bit when the comparison is suspended) with elaboration at the first use; the suite ran the family with the default thresholds, and with the host depth bound at 0 under the default set only.}
\li{\strong{The mode \code{shared}} was gated by the hostile suite in process only; the launcher gates and the corpora ran the default mode.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{12. The patch}

\p{One unified diff and one archive, not applied and not published: the diff changes 18 text files of \code{rlvm-machine}, the archive holds the 46 gzipped exports. Checked when it was made: it applies to the main tree of 01:50 UTC in the night that followed 2026-11-25 (which carries the patches of [the package on the re-check](rep-MO-1)), and that tree with the diff and the archive is, file for file, the private tree that was gated.}

\table{
\tr{\th{File of \code{rlvm-machine}}\th{What}}
\tr{\td{\code{Machine.java}}\td{the mode and its fields, \code{enterER} in place, \code{erDefers}, the stamp of a declaration (\code{beginDecl}, \code{reset}), the cut of the driver gives a comparison's mark back, the statistics}}
\tr{\td{\code{Debug.java}}\td{the re-computation of a memo hit (\code{memocheck}) leaves the mark as it found it}}
\tr{\td{\code{Pairs.java}}\td{local entries: \code{status} reads one of another declaration as absent, \code{add} writes one for a marked comparison, the search by expression}}
\tr{\td{\code{Conv.java}}\td{the mark of a comparison (\code{convScoped} and its frame, the continuation \code{\verb~|k_conv2_11~}), the marks before the two probes, the eager-only event in place, no shortcut under the bit, the cheap fold test, the search after a failure under the bit}}
\tr{\td{\code{Reduce.java}}\td{the K rule: \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} hands the two types on, \code{\verb~|k_iota4~} and its slow path ask the plain comparison, the new continuation \code{\verb~|k_iota14~} with its frame key and its line in the resume switch}}
\tr{\td{\code{MachineSession.java}}\td{the mode, the run alone before a rejection, the accounting; nothing is forgotten after an eager declaration in place}}
\tr{\td{\code{MachineEngine.java}, \code{MachineProvider.java}}\td{the setting \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=local|shared|deferred~} (system property \code{\verb~|rlvm.machine.eager-mode~}), the statistics \code{\verb~|machine.eager.mode / marked / localWrites / localDead / kRetries / kRetriesT / sameAsked / sameHits~}}}
\tr{\td{\code{Layout.java}, \code{Op.java}}\td{the status bit \code{LOCAL} with its stamp, the bit \code{\verb~|ER_KEV~}; the text of the switch}}
\tr{\td{\code{\verb~|h10/force-inline.txt~}}\td{\code{\verb~|Conv.convScoped~} (one call site in the hot path)}}
\tr{\td{\code{README.md}}\td{the paragraph on eager reduction}}
\tr{\td{tests}\td{\code{SHostileEagerTest} (rewritten for the three modes), \code{M4LocalPairsTest} (new), \code{M6FacadeTest} (the mode; the probe test names the deferred mode), \code{SHostile} (the notes)}}
\tr{\td{test resources}\td{\code{\verb~|s/manifest.tsv~} (46 rows, the note \code{eager-accept} on 8 rows, the note \code{eager-hard} removed from 20, the header), \code{\verb~|s/gen/batchM.py~}, the 46 exports}}
}

\p{\strong{What the integrator must know.}}
\ul{
\li{\strong{Not touched}: \code{Infer.java}, \code{Code.java}, \code{Values.java}, \code{Eval.java}. The two places that check an eager argument are unchanged: they ask \code{\verb~|Machine.erDefers~}, which is false in place, and go on to the comparison under the bit that was always there.}
\li{\strong{\code{Reduce.java} is shared with the package on the K rule after a let application} (package MO-2 of the same day), which rewrites the same function: this patch changes the last line of \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} (the frame now carries the two types), all of \code{\verb~|k_iota4~}, adds two methods after it, one frame key and two lines in the resume switch. The check of the let rule at the top of \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} is not touched.}
\li{\strong{The two patches must not be merged by \code{patch}.} Applied after the other package's diffs, this patch's hunk on \code{\verb~|k_iota4~} goes through with fuzz, and the result compiles and is wrong: the tail that the other package adds to \code{\verb~|k_iota4~} (in its exact-let mode the question of the K rule is asked again under the other reading of a let variable) lands in this patch's new continuation \code{\verb~|k_iota14~}, where it never runs. In a scratch copy merged that way the machine accepted eight hostile rows of the other package that the official checker rejects and rejected eight that it accepts; \code{SHostileTest} and \code{SHostileLetTest} fail on it, and it was reported on the team's board when it was seen. Neither patch alone has the fault. What this says about integrating is [an insight of its own](ins-GK2B). Merged by hand - the other package's tail as one method that all three ends of the K comparison call (\code{\verb~|k_iota4~}, its slow path under the bit, \code{\verb~|k_iota14~}) - the copy passes the default test group of the module (464 tests, no failure), \code{SHostileSlowTest} (56 switch sets by 1,387 rows, nothing unexpected) and the hostile step of the launcher gate through the jars (1,387 rows: official verdict 1,311, accept of a kind T row 60, the 12 known rows, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4). Nothing else was gated on the merged copy: no corpus, no mutants, not the machine's gate, no timing. Handed over beside the patch: a variant of the diff that applies after the other package's two diffs as they were at 01:20 UTC, and the script that makes the merged copy again and stops if the other package's tail is not where it expects it. The paragraph of the README and the manifest are rejected hunks there (two independent paragraphs; rows appended at the same place).}
\li{\strong{\code{MachineSession.check0}} merges mechanically, and the two repeated runs do not meet: this patch's run alone before a rejection needs the comparison in place, and the other package's run in exact-let mode has no switch, so it is not in place. For the same reason a unit that needs the exact-let run and enters eager reduction is still declined after both patches (the rule M6-F26, in a run without the switch). With the comparison in place the switch \code{eager} could stay on in that run; that was not built and not tested.}
\li{\strong{The manifest} is a file that every package on the hostile suite edits; the patch was made against the manifest with the marks of the package on the re-check. The tool that made the rows reads the manifest it is given, so it can be made again against a later one.}
\li{\strong{The fallback}: \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=deferred~} is the published mechanism to the tick (section 6); it stays in the sources with its tests.}
\li{\strong{The notes of the journal that describe the mechanism} ([eager reduction on the machine](design-eager-reduction), [the switch reference](design-switches-document), the open problem on the size of a probe, [prob-HC9J](prob-HC9J)) describe the deferred mode; they are not changed by this package and need a revision when the patch is applied: the probe budget then belongs to a mode that is not the default, and the problem of the large probe no longer concerns a default run.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{13. Not measured, not done}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the architecture of the arena's runner, and the arena's metric (instructions): nothing was measured; the arena's 270-test procedure in its container was not run (the 230 published tests ran through both launchers).}
\li{\strong{The mode \code{shared}} through the launcher gates and the mutant corpora: it ran the hostile suite in process (three switch sets), the corpora in count runs and the guard.}
\li{\strong{The driver's invariance suite} (verdict against the number of workers and the interleaving of loading and checking): not run; the change is inside one declaration's check and one session's table, and the eight corpora accept with 8, 4 and 1 workers.}
\li{\strong{The strict policy and the reference engine by name}: not touched by the patch (a strict machine has no switch), and not run again.}
\li{\strong{The mutant corpora in the machine's own harness} (\code{\verb~|report.sh mutants~}): not run; the launcher gate ran the 17,775 mutants through both launchers.}
\li{\strong{A cut segment whose comparisons were suspended}, in a declaration with a mode-dependent pair: no row (section 11).}
\li{\strong{The patch together with the other two packages of this day}: it was gated on the tree that carries the package on the re-check. With the package on the K rule after a let application only a hand-merged scratch copy was tested, with the module's suites and the hostile step of the launcher gate (section 12): no corpus, no mutants, not the machine's gate, no timing.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{14. The review of the first candidate, and the second candidate: the union rule}

\p{Sections 3 to 13 describe the first candidate, the mode \code{local}, as it was built and gated; they are kept as the record of what was tried. Two adversarial reviews of that build, [from the source](rep-RV-3A) and [from the outside](rep-RV-3B), refused it within an hour: no accept of a statement that is not derivable, and wrong rejections on hand-made and generated exports. This section says what fell, what replaced it, and what the replacement was measured to do. The second candidate is a new private build of a new private copy (the main tree of 22:46 UTC with the patch of this section); nothing of it is applied or published.}

\subtree{
\title{14.1 What fell}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The premise of rules 5 and 7}: that the machine can know, or reconstruct, what the official success cache holds. The official cache holds every sub-pair that a plain comparison of the declaration compared (the wrapper \code{\verb~|is_def_eq~} caches, and it is what the argument loops, the comparison of two proofs by their types and structure eta call). A machine whose plain mode answers by a shortcut - a skipped proof position, a skipped unread position, a same-head probe that refutes a literal before the last arguments - never makes those comparisons, in the run alone no more than in the first run (findings F1 of RV-3A and F2 to F4 of RV-3B). The search by expression cannot find what was never entered, and where the entry exists under other records its walk is bounded (40 host frames, 256 nodes: F2 of RV-3A, F5 of RV-3B).}
\li{\strong{Rule 6 and the sentence "no memo word of a reduction needs a stamp"} (section 2). The rule made a recursor reduce under the bit whenever it reduces in plain mode. That is wrong in both directions. A recursor that the flag leaves stuck must stay stuck: the official kernel compares two stuck recursors argument by argument, and a reduct against a stuck one is false (F1 of RV-3B, with the arena's prelude: two K recursors of which plain mode reduces one). And a K comparison can be true under the flag only (F3 of RV-3A, with a Nat function whose body does not compute): then the memo of the reduction - the reduct, or "stuck" - was written in one mode and read in the other, inside a declaration and across declarations.}
\li{\strong{The claim of section 10} that every exception is an accept. The reviews show rejections: 10 of the 58 exports of RV-3A, and 199 of the 3,681 generated exports of RV-3B that the first candidate was run on here (RV-3B counts 200 of 3,683), each an official accept that the published build accepts.}
\li{\strong{Schedule}: the first candidate's verdict on four exports of 300 theorems depended on which session had checked an earlier declaration (F6 of RV-3B); the published build's does not.}
\li{\strong{The mode \code{shared}} as a variant: it rejects 190 official accepts of the generated corpus (RV-3B). It is gone.}
}

\p{What stood: the reading of the official source (section 1), the bit in place, the marks and the local entries (rules 1 to 4), the counts on the corpora, and the observation that no real declaration, and no mutant of one that the second review could make (1,490), does anything under the flag that the flag alone allows.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.2 The union rule}

\p{Whether the official kernel answers a pair under the flag from its cache depends on which plain comparisons it happened to make before, and a machine with shortcuts does not make them. Any rule that guesses the content of that cache rejects official accepts. The standing policies decide the ambiguous zone: everything the official checker accepts is accepted; a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through the order of its cache may be accepted; a statement that is not derivable is never accepted. So the second candidate does not guess. \strong{Under the bit a pair is true if the eager procedure or the plain procedure shows it.} Both are sound conversions, so their union accepts no statement that is not derivable; it does not reject a pair that either can show; and it gives up the official rejection of a pair that is first asked under the flag and is true in plain mode. Setting: \code{\verb~|-X machine.eager-mode=union~} (the default) or \code{deferred} (the published mechanism, unchanged).}

\ol{
\li{\strong{In place, official order first} (rules 1 and 2 of section 3, unchanged): the comparison of an eager argument runs where it stands under the bit, without the shortcuts of plain mode.}
\li{\strong{Asked again in plain mode.} A comparison that ends false under the bit is asked again with the bit cleared, at its own level (\code{\verb~|Conv.convScoped~}, \code{\verb~|Conv.unionSlow~}): the innermost failing pair first; if its plain run fails too, false goes to the pair it is part of, which is asked again in plain mode itself, because the official cache can hold a pair at any level. The plain run is the machine's plain procedure as it is, shortcuts and entries included.}
\li{\strong{Two stuck recursors.} More reduction can lose a pair: a recursor that the machine reduced where the official kernel left it stuck - through a shortcut of plain mode, a memo, an entry that the official cache does not hold - against a recursor that stays stuck is false for both procedures, where the official kernel compares the two stuck applications argument by argument. So a pair of applications of one recursor (same levels, same number of arguments) that is false both ways is compared as it stands, argument by argument (\code{\verb~|Conv.unionArgs~}). That is congruence: valid whatever the recursors reduce to, and not bounded by a budget. It sees a pair that a comparison is called with; a pair of recursors that only appears inside the unfolding loop of one comparison is beyond it (section 14.8, item 2).}
\li{\strong{The other way round after an eager-only event.} In a declaration in which eager reduction took a branch of its own, a plain comparison that ends false is asked under the bit, and then as in item 3: the official cache may hold the pair from a comparison under the flag, under other value records than the ones in hand. On real declarations this never runs (no eager-only event).}
\li{\strong{The K rule stays in its mode.} The comparison of the K rule is the one place where a true answer changes a reduction. Under the bit it is the eager procedure and nothing else (no union inside it): a recursor that the flag leaves stuck stays stuck in the eager attempt of the pair it is part of, and reduces in the plain attempt of that pair. If the comparison was marked - it passed a point where the modes may part, or read a local entry - every reduction in progress that it is part of writes \strong{no memo word} (\code{\verb~|Machine.kDep~}, set in \code{\verb~|Reduce.k_iota4~}, read in \code{\verb~|whnfDone~} and \code{\verb~|coreDone~}, cleared when a comparison starts). So no reduct and no "stuck" of one mode is read in the other or in a later declaration.}
\li{\strong{A false is one mode's answer.} Under the bit no false entry of the pair table is read or written: the memo of a false comparison as before, and now also the failure cache of same-head argument comparisons (the machine then makes the comparison that the official kernel would skip, which can only add a true answer). After an eager-only event of a declaration its local false entries are not read at all (\code{\verb~|Pairs.status~}): a pair that only eager reduction shows may have entered the table since.}
\li{\strong{Marks and local entries} as rules 3 and 4 of section 3. A session is forgotten after a declaration with an eager-only event, as the published mechanism does, so that the terms it checked are checked again, with their eager comparisons, by whoever meets them; the shared check facts were never published once a declaration had entered eager reduction.}
}

\p{\strong{Removed}: the search by expression (rule 5), the plain comparison inside the K rule and its mark bit (rule 6), the run alone (rule 7), the modes \code{local} and \code{shared}. No probe, no deferred comparison, no second run, no decline: that part of the first candidate is unchanged.}

\p{\strong{Why not a stamp on the memo words} (the two options of RV-3A for finding F3). A memo word is a value reference without a free bit, and the twelve flag bits of a record are taken; a stamp would have needed a side table or a change of the record layout in the hottest path of the machine. Not writing the word needs one test where a memo is written. The numbers of section 14.5 decide: on the eight corpora 0 to 109 comparisons of the K rule run under the flag (Mathlib: 109 among 116,561 comparisons under the flag) and none of them is marked, so there is nothing for a stamp to distinguish in a real declaration; what the rule costs is in plain mode, where 0 to 5,304 comparisons of the K rule per corpus are marked and 0 to 266,288 memo words are not written: at most +0.67 \% of the machine's ticks (navier-stokes-euler), +0.06 \% or less on the other seven. So: no memo, no stamp.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.3 Every memo that a reduction or a comparison leaves, and the mode}

\table{
\tr{\th{Memo}\th{What the second candidate does, and why it gives no wrong rejection and no accept of a statement that is not derivable}}
\tr{\td{pair table, success (\code{POS})}\td{Written and read in both modes. A success is a valid conversion whichever procedure showed it, so reading it can only add a true answer. Marked: local to its declaration, as the official cache entry it stands for; this also keeps a pair that only one mode shows out of the K comparisons of later declarations.}}
\tr{\td{pair table, the memo of a false comparison (\code{NEG})}\td{Plain mode only, as before. Marked: local, and not read after an eager-only event of the declaration. Not marked: the comparison passed no point where the modes part, so the eager procedure fails on it as well.}}
\tr{\td{pair table, the failure cache of same-head arguments (\code{FAILED})}\td{Plain mode only (new). The official kernel shares it between the modes; not reading it makes the machine compare arguments where the official kernel goes on to unfold, which can add a true answer and cannot remove one.}}
\tr{\td{memo words of reductions (\code{\verb~|M_WHNF~}, \code{\verb~|M_CORE~} and the flag of a full core result)}\td{Not written while a marked comparison of the K rule is part of the reduction in progress (item 5). Otherwise the comparison of the K rule passed no point where the modes part and read no local entry; the word is the same in both modes - except that the machine's plain mode may have answered that comparison by a shortcut where the official order answers false. Then a reduct of plain mode is read under the bit. That can make the eager attempt of a pair fail; the plain attempt of the same pair reduces the other side by the same shortcut, and where it cannot, item 3 compares the two applications as they stand. Rows: \code{\verb~|rv3a-kshort-*~}, \code{\verb~|mo3u-kcopy-*~}.}}
\tr{\td{the unfolding of a constant (\code{\verb~|M_UNF~}), the value of a suspension (\code{\verb~|M_VAL~}), the parts of a binder (\code{\verb~|M_DOM~}, \code{\verb~|M_COD~}, \code{\verb~|M_CODE~}), the constructor form of a string literal}\td{No comparison takes part in them: the same in both modes.}}
\tr{\td{types and check stamps (\code{\verb~|M_TYPE~}, \code{\verb~|M_TSTAMP~}, the level of a binder type, the front cache of inference)}\td{A type that was inferred is a type of the term in either mode. A check stamp says that a term passed check mode; what a later declaration loses by not checking it again is the entries of its comparisons, which the union does not depend on in plain direction (item 2) and, after an eager-only event, the session is forgotten (item 7).}}
\tr{\td{the shared check facts (\code{pchk})}\td{Not published once the declaration has entered eager reduction (unchanged from the published build).}}
\tr{\td{derived rules and case trees of definitions (the switches \code{\verb~|k2~}, \code{casetrees}, \code{lazycases}, \code{directrules}), fact records of constants}\td{Facts of a definition's body. Rules are neither derived nor used under the bit, and a K-like major premise is never split (unchanged).}}
\tr{\td{the budget memory of the same-head probe}\td{Says whether a probe runs, never what a comparison answers.}}
}

\p{This table is from reading the layout of the value records and the places that write a memo word; it was not checked by a tool. The convention part of the machine's gate compares the pair entries, the ticks and the records under four host depth bounds (section 14.7).}
}

