\p{\strong{No comparison under the flag is false on any of the eight corpora}: the plain procedure is never asked, no pair of recursors is compared as it stands, no eager-only event happens, no session is forgotten and no declaration is run twice. On real declarations the second candidate is the first one without its three unused rules, plus the memo words that a marked comparison of the K rule keeps from being written. Against the first candidate the ticks are +0.00 to +0.06 \% on seven corpora and +0.67 \% on navier-stokes-euler, where 5,304 of 11,869 plain comparisons of the K rule are marked; against the published build they are -0.09 to -1.24 \% on seven corpora and +0.65 \% on navier-stokes-euler. The units that the reference engine decides are the same as for the first candidate (con-leche 2 to 1, cslib 11 to 7, Mathlib 64 to 60 against the published build; none of what is left is a decline of eager reduction). The marks of the K rule are the cheap over-approximation of section 3 (an open pair that a Nat folding may answer); a narrower test, or carrying the state in the frames of the reductions instead of one flag until the next comparison starts, would lower the count of unwritten words and was not built.}
}

