\p{\strong{The executable passes the rule}: geometric mean 0.993, the largest ratio 1.006 (con-leche), the CPU time of a pass between -1.1 and +0.2 \%, the peak resident set within 9 MiB of the published build on every corpus. \strong{The JVM launcher does not pass it cleanly.} Its geometric mean is 0.990 and seven corpora lie between 0.952 and 1.006, but navier-stokes-euler is at \strong{1.031}, over the limit of 3 \% by a tenth of a point, in three rounds taken while the load average of the other cores was 87. The repeat of that corpus with six rounds at a load average of 35 gives 3,214.0 against 3,251.0 ms, a ratio of \strong{1.012}, with the CPU time of a pass at +1.7 \% and the peak resident set equal (2,324 and 2,321 MiB). So by the letter the first window fails the rule on one corpus and the repeat passes it; what both say is that this corpus is slower on the JVM launcher by 1 to 3 \%, and not on the executable (0.980, CPU +0.1 \%). It is the corpus on which the rule of item 5 of section 14.2 costs most: 5,304 marked plain comparisons of the K rule, 266,288 memo words not written, +0.65 \% of the machine's ticks. A way to remove that cost was not built: write the word in plain mode as before, keep a list of the words written under a marked comparison of the K rule, and clear them when a declaration reaches an eager argument (the only place where they could be read in the other mode); on a declaration without an eager argument that costs one list entry per word. On the other seven corpora and on the executable the second candidate is the first one: no change that the guard can see.}
}

