\p{The suite has 1,433 rows now: the 1,320 of section 5, the 58 rows of RV-3A and the 42 of RV-3B with the reviewers' marks, and 13 rows of this section's generator (\src{rlvm-machine/src/test/resources/s/gen/batchU.py}). Two notes are new: \code{eager-accept} now names every kind T row of the family that the machine accepts under the default set (60 rows: 37 of the 101, 23 of the reviews' rows), and \code{deferred-reject} names six official accepts that the mode \code{deferred}, the published mechanism, rejects; the tests of that mode leave them out.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.8 What the union does not cover, and the reviewers' notes}

\ol{
\li{\strong{Agreement.} The union accepts every pair that is true in plain mode wherever it is asked under the flag. On hostile exports that is most official rejections of the family: 37 of 41 on the suite's rows, 1,418 of 2,105 on the generated corpus (the published mechanism: 1,384). They are derivable statements; the policy allows the accept. The first candidate's point - the official rejection on a pair first asked under the flag - is given up.}
\li{\strong{Demonstrated: a recursor that the machine's plain mode reduced and the official kernel leaves stuck, behind a definition.} The third step of the rule (item 3 of section 14.2) repairs the pair of two recursor applications when a comparison is called with it. Three exports of this package put the two recursors behind definitions (\code{\verb~|S.w1 := R~}, \code{\verb~|S.w4 := R4~}, or \code{\verb~|S.j1 R~} against \code{\verb~|S.j4 R4~}): the pair of recursors then appears only inside the unfolding loop of one comparison, both procedures read the reduct of \code{R}, the third step does not see two recursor applications, and \strong{the second candidate rejects three official accepts} (section 14.9). The published build rejects the same three, so they are no regression; they are the limit of this candidate, found by its author's rows after its gates had started, and reported on the team's board at once. The root is not eager reduction: the machine's K rule fires in plain mode where the official one does not - a shortcut answers its comparison and leaves a pair entry and a memo of the reduction - and then more reduction loses a pair against a recursor that nobody can reduce.}
\li{\strong{What would close it, and what does not.} (a) Not remembering any reduction that passed a comparison of the K rule was measured in a counting copy: 6.7 M memo words not written and \strong{+15.4 \% of the machine's ticks on navier-stokes-euler}; it is not offered. (b) A third step that runs the whole pair again with the K rule off (no K recursor fires, no memo word of a reduction is read or written) instead of comparing two applications as they stand would see through definitions, projections and other recursors; it needs a test at each of the seven places that read a memo word of a reduction, which are on the hottest path, and a guard of its own. (c) The root repair is a K rule whose comparison is the official one in both modes: no shortcut inside it, and no pair entry read in it that a shortcut wrote. That changes the plain mode of every declaration and belongs to whoever owns the K rule there. None of the three was built. Even with (b), an official accept that needs one recursor reduced by a true K comparison and another one left stuck inside one reduction stays outside the argument.}
\li{\strong{Cost on hostile input.} After an eager-only event every false comparison of the declaration is computed in both modes at every level, and no false answer is remembered: exponential in the depth of a failing comparison in the worst case. The result is a tick bound and a decline, which the reference engine decides, never a verdict. Not measured on a constructed worst case.}
\li{\strong{The marks} are still an over-approximation by a cheap test (section 10, last item); they now decide the life of entries and whether a reduction is remembered, no longer a verdict.}
}

\p{\strong{On the notes of RV-3A.} The proof-by-type shortcut that stays on under the bit: under the union a shortcut under the bit that answers true can only add an accept of a derivable statement; rule 2 no longer carries a verdict, it only keeps the eager attempt the official one where that is free. The Prop mask of structure eta as one more shortcut that hides a sub-pair: the pair that is asked under the flag later is shown by its own plain run, whatever hid it. Pair entries under several host depth bounds: the convention part of the machine's gate compares them (section 14.7). The reviewer's suggestion patch (the run alone without the shortcuts; the K rule asking the plain comparison after every false answer under the bit): both are subsumed and neither is kept - there is no run alone, and the K rule asks nothing again.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.9 Wrong rejections of the published build, found on the way}

\p{Reported on the team's board when they were seen. All are exports with the arena's prelude and closed terms, built against the union rule (generator \code{batchU.py}): \code{\verb~|R = Eq.rec 1500 e~} with \code{\verb~|e : X = Y h~}, and \code{\verb~|R4 = Eq.rec (S.inner3 1500) e4~} with \code{\verb~|e4 : X = Y4 h~}, \code{Y4} through a copy of the function \code{G}. Officially no K comparison succeeds in either mode (closed pairs: the fold answers first), both recursors are stuck and equal argument by argument. The machine's plain mode answers the K comparison of \code{R} by its same-head shortcut and reduces it; it cannot reduce \code{R4} (\code{G} against its copy), and its same-head probe of the two applications runs out of budget on the minor premises.}

\table{
\tr{\th{Export, official accept}\th{Published build}\th{Second candidate}}
\tr{\td{\code{mo3u-kcopy-eager-alone-ok}: \code{\verb~|R = R4~} under \code{eagerReduce} alone (the published mechanism runs the deferred comparison with the shortcuts)}\td{\strong{reject}}\td{accept}}
\tr{\td{\code{mo3u-kcopy-other-minor-plain-then-eager-ok}, \code{-plain-declaration-then-eager-ok}, \code{-under-recursor-ok}: a plain comparison reduces \code{R} first, then the pair under \code{eagerReduce}}\td{\strong{reject}}\td{accept (item 3 of section 14.2)}}
\tr{\td{\code{mo3u-kcopy-wrapped-plain-then-eager-ok}, \code{-wrapped-one-side-plain-then-eager-ok}, \code{-wrapped-applied-plain-then-eager-ok}: the same with the recursors behind definitions}\td{\strong{reject}}\td{\strong{reject}}}
\tr{\td{\code{mo3u-kcopy-other-minor-plain-twice-ok}: no \code{eagerReduce} anywhere}\td{\strong{reject}}\td{\strong{reject}}}
}

\p{Eight wrong rejections of the published build, of which the second candidate repairs four. The last one is a wrong rejection of the plain mode; the third step of the union rule (item 3 of section 14.2) applied after every false plain comparison would repair it, at a cost on the corpora that was not measured. The four that the candidate does not repair are not rows of the suite (they would need the mark of a known wrong rejection, whose count the launcher gate lets go down only); they are handed over beside the patch with their official verdicts. The four that the candidate repairs and two exports of the first review (the pair first under the flag, then in plain mode, [section 4 there](rep-RV-3A)) are the six rows with the note \code{deferred-reject}.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.10 The patch of the second candidate}

\p{One unified diff and one archive, not applied and not published; they replace the patch of section 12. The diff changes 19 text files of \code{rlvm-machine} (the 18 of section 12 and the new generator \code{batchU.py}); the archive holds 159 gzipped exports (46 and 13 of this package, 58 and 42 of the two reviews). Checked when it was made: it applies to the main tree of 04:36 UTC in the night that followed 2026-11-25 with no fuzz allowed, and that tree with the diff and the archive is, file for file, the private tree that was gated. \strong{Against the first candidate}: gone are \code{\verb~|Pairs.samePos~}, \code{\verb~|Conv.recFlagSlow~}, \code{\verb~|Reduce.kLeaveSlow~} and \code{\verb~|k_iota14~} with its frame key, the bit \code{\verb~|Op.ER_KEV~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.erAlone~} and the run alone of \code{\verb~|MachineSession.check0~}, the modes \code{local} and \code{shared}; new are \code{\verb~|Conv.convEnd~}, \code{\verb~|unionSlow~} and \code{\verb~|unionArgs~} with the frames \code{\verb~|CONV2[12]~} and \code{\verb~|CONV2[13]~}, \code{\verb~|Machine.kDep~}, the fourth word of the frame \code{\verb~|IOTA[4]~} with \code{\verb~|Reduce.kEnter~}, \code{\verb~|kSignal~} and \code{\verb~|kDepSkip~}, the two tests in \code{\verb~|whnfDone~} and \code{\verb~|coreDone~}, the failure cache neither read nor written under the bit (\code{\verb~|Conv.lstepBoth~}), \code{\verb~|Pairs.localSlow~} after an eager-only event, and the statistics \code{\verb~|machine.eager.unionAsked / unionT / unionBackAsked / unionBackT / unionArgs / unionArgsT / kDepSkips~}. Still not touched: \code{Infer.java}, \code{Code.java}, \code{Values.java}, \code{Eval.java}, \code{Cargs.java}. \strong{With the package on the K rule after a let application}: applied after that package's two diffs, this patch's hunk on \code{\verb~|k_iota3~} and \code{\verb~|k_iota4~} is rejected by \code{patch} (it no longer goes through with fuzz, section 12), as are the paragraph of the README and the manifest. A script makes the merged copy - the other package's tail stays in \code{\verb~|k_iota4~}, this patch's lines go around it - and a variant of the diff that applies after the other package's diffs is handed over. On the merged copy: the default test group of the module (464 tests, no failure), \code{SHostileSlowTest} (56 switch sets by 1,500 rows, nothing unexpected), and the hostile step of the launcher gate through the jars (1,500 rows: official verdict 1,372, accept of a kind T row 112, the 12 known rows, wrong accept 0, wrong reject 0, declined 4). Nothing else was gated on the merged copy. The generators of the two reviews are not in the patch (they name the reviewers' working files); their rows are, with the reviewers' marks. The journal notes named at the end of section 12 need the same revision as said there.}
}

\subtree{
\title{14.11 Not measured, not done (second candidate)}

\ul{
\li{\strong{x86-64}, the arena's metric (instructions) and the arena's 270-test procedure in its container: not run, as for the first candidate.}
\li{\strong{The driver's invariance suite}: not run. What stands in for it: the reviewers' schedule family (48 exports, 30 runs each over 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers and both loadings) and the real mutants with the default number of workers.}
\li{\strong{The 46 mutants of the heavy slice} of the second review (a changed literal of one declaration of con-leche makes both kernels compute for minutes and gigabytes): left out.}
\li{\strong{A constructed worst case} of the cost after an eager-only event (every false comparison in both modes at every level), and \strong{an export with a mixed derivation through the K rule} (section 14.8, items 3 and 4): not built.}
\li{\strong{The wrong rejection of the plain mode} (section 14.9): not repaired, and the cost of comparing two recursor applications as they stand after every false plain comparison was not measured.}
\li{\strong{The cost on navier-stokes-euler} (+0.65 \% of ticks; +1 to +3 \% on the JVM launcher): none of the three ways to lower it was built - a narrower mark of the K rule, its state in the frames of the reductions, or the list of marked memo words that is cleared at an eager argument (section 14.6).}
\li{\strong{The three ways to close the wrong rejections that are left} (section 14.8, item 3): none was built; the four exports are handed over with their official verdicts.}
\li{\strong{The mode \code{deferred}} keeps the wrong rejections of the published mechanism (six rows of the suite carry the note); nothing was done about them.}
\li{\strong{The kind of the nine rows with a Nat function that does not compute}: the first review reads them as kind T and asks for the maintainer's confirmation; this package took the reviewer's marks as they are.}
\li{\strong{The patch together with the package on the K rule after a let application}: the module's suites and the hostile step on a hand-merged copy only.}
}
}
}
